A|                                       TEASER                                       
O|
S|               EXT. COW PASTURE - DAY                                               
O|
N|               Deep blue sky overhead. Fat, scuddy clouds. Below them,              
N|               black and white cows graze the rolling hills. This could be          
N|               one of those California “It’s The Cheese” commercials.               
O|
N|               Except those commercials don’t normally focus on cow shit.           
N,G|               We do.  TILT DOWN to a fat, round PATTY drying olive drab in         
N|               the sun.  Flies buzz. Peaceful and quiet. Until...                   
N|               ... ZOOOM! WHEELS plow right through the shit with a SPLAT.          
O|
N,G|               NEW ANGLE - AN RV                                                    
O|
N|               Is speeding smack-dab through the pasture, no road in sight.         
N|               A bit out of place, to say the least. It’s an old 70’s era           
N|               Winnebago with chalky white paint and Bondo spots. A bumper          
N|               sticker for the Good Sam Club is stuck to the back.                  
O|
N|               The Winnebago galumphs across the landscape, scattering cows.        
N|               It catches a wheel and sprays a rooster tail of red dirt.            
O|
S|               INT. WINNEBAGO - DAY                                                 
O|
N|               Inside, the DRIVER’s knuckles cling white to the wheel. He’s         
N|               got the pedal flat. Scared, breathing fast. His eyes bug             
N|               wide behind the faceplate of his gas mask.                           
O|
N|               Oh, by the way, he’s wearing a GAS MASK. That, and white             
N|               jockey UNDERPANTS. Nothing else.                                     
O|
N|               Buckled in the seat beside him lolls a clothed PASSENGER,            
N|               also wearing a gas mask. Blood streaks down from his ear,            
N|               blotting his T-shirt. He’s passed out cold.                          
N|               Behind them, the interior is a wreck. Beakers and buckets            
N|               and flasks -- some kind of ad-hoc CHEMICAL LAB -- spill their        
N|               noxious contents with every bump we hit. Yellow-brown liquid         
N|               washes up and down the floor. It foams in a scum around...           
O|
N|               ... Two DEAD BODIES. Two freshly deceased Mexican guys               
N|               tumble like rag dolls, bumping into each other.                      
O|
N|               Completing this picture is the blizzard of MONEY. A Von’s            
N|               bag lies leaking twenties. Fifteen, twenty grand in cash             
N|               wafts around in the air or floats in the nasty brown soup.           
N,G|               CLOSE on the driver’s eyes. He’s panting like a steam                
N|               engine.  His mask FOGS UP until finally he can’t see.                
O|
O|
O|                                                                      2.            
O|
S|               EXT. COW PASTURE - CONTINUOUS                                        
O|
N|               The Winnebago comes roaring over a berm and down into a deep         
N|               gully.  Too deep. BAM!  The front bumper bottoms out,                
N|               burying itself. WAAAAAAH!  The rear wheels spin air.                 
O|
N|               The engine cuts off. Silence again.  The Winnie’s door kicks         
N|               open and out stumbles underpants man. He yanks off his gas           
N|               mask, lets it drop.                                                  
N|               He’s forty years old. Receding hairline. A bit pasty.                
N|               He’s not a guy who makes a living working with his hands.            
N|               He’s not a guy we’d pay attention to if we passed him on the         
N|               street.  But right now, at this moment, in this pasture?             
N|               Right now, we’d step the fuck out of his way.                        
O|
N|               Underpants man looks at the RV. End of the line for that.            
N|               He listens hard. Out of the silence, we hear... SIRENS.              
O|
N|               They’re faint, a few miles off -- but growing louder. Our            
N|               guy knows he’s boned with a capital B. He HOLDS HIS BREATH           
N|               and leaps back inside the RV.                                        
O|
S|               INT. WINNEBAGO - CONTINUOUS                                          
O|
N|               A chrome 9mm is clutched in the hand of one of the dead              
N|               Mexicans. Underpants grabs it, tucks it in his waistband.            
O|
N|               His unconscious passenger, still strapped in his seat, lets          
N|               out a groan. Underpants leans past him, yanks open the glove         
N|               box.  He comes up with a WALLET and a tiny Sony CAMCORDER.           
O|
S|               EXT. COW PASTURE - CONTINUOUS                                        
N|               Ducking outside, he starts breathing again. A short sleeve           
N|               DRESS SHIRT on a hanger dangles from the Winnebago’s awning.         
N|               Underpants pulls it on. He finds a clip-on tie in the                
N|               pocket, snaps it to his collar. No trousers, unfortunately.          
O|
N|               He licks his fingers, slicks his hair down with his hands.           
N|               He’s looking almost pulled together now -- at least from the         
N|               waist-up. All the while, the sirens are getting LOUDER.              
O|
N|               Underpants figures out how to turn on the camcorder. He              
N|               twists the little screen around so he can see himself in it.         
N|               Framing himself waist-up, he takes a moment to gather his            
N|               thoughts... then presses RECORD.                                     
O|
O|
O|                                                                      3.            
O|
C|                                   UNDERPANTS MAN                                   
U|                         My name is Walter Hartwell White.                          
U|                         I live at 308 Belmont Avenue,                              
U|                         Ontario, California 91764. I am of                         
U|                         sound mind. To all law enforcement                         
U|                         entities, this is not an admission                         
U|                         of guilt. I’m speaking now to my                           
U|                         family.                                                    
P|                             (swallows hard)                                        
U|                         Skyler... you are... the love of my                        
U|                         life.  I hope you know that.                               
U|                         Walter Junior. You’re my big man.                          
U|                         I should have told you things, both                        
U|                         of you. I should have said things.                         
U|                         But I love you both so much. And                           
U|                         our unborn child. And I just want                          
U|                         you to know that these... things                           
U|                         you’re going to learn about me in                          
U|                         the coming days. These things.                             
U|                         I just want you to know that...                            
U|                         no matter what it may look like...                         
U|                         I had all three of you in my heart.                        
N,I|               The sirens are WAILING now, on top of us. WALTER WHITE, the          
N,I|               underpants man, turns off the camcorder. He carefully sets           
N|               it on a bare patch of ground by his feet. Next to it he sets         
N|               his wallet, lying open where it can be seen.                         
N,G|               CLOSE ON the wallet -- a photo ID card is visible. Walt’s            
N|               smiling face is on it. It identifies him as a teacher at             
N|               J.P. Wynne High School, Ontario Unified School District.             
O|
N|               Walt pulls the chrome pistol from the back of his waistband,         
N|               aiming it across the tall weeds. It glints hard in the sun.          
O|
N|               Flashing red LIGHT BARS speed into view, skimming the tops of        
N|               the weeds. Heading straight for us.                                  
N,G|               Walt stands tall in his underpants, not flinching. Off him,          
N|               ready to shoot the first cop he sees...                              
O|
A|                                     END TEASER                                     
O|
O|
O|                                                                      4.            
O|
A|                                       ACT ONE                                      
O|
S|               EXT. WHITE HOUSE - NIGHT                                             
O|
N|               No president ever slept here. No millionaire ever visited.           
N|               This is a three-bedroom RANCHER in a modest neighborhood.            
N|               Weekend trips to Home Depot keep it looking tidy, but it’ll          
N|               never make the cover of “Architectural Digest.”                      
O|
N,Y|               We’re in Ontario, California -- the Inland Empire. LEGEND:           
Y|               “ONE MONTH EARLIER.”                                                 
O|
S|               INT. WHITE HOUSE - MASTER BEDROOM - NIGHT                            
O|
N,I|               Dark and silent. SKYLER WHITE, late 30s, sleeps peacefully.          
N|               Beside her, her husband Walter is wide awake.                        
O|
N|               Walt reaches over and presses a button on his Sharper Image          
N|               alarm clock. It projects the time in glowing blue numbers on         
N|               the cottage cheese ceiling: 5:02 AM.                                 
O|
N|               Walt lies motionless. Brain churning.  He presses the button         
N|               again, staring straight up. 5:02 turns to 5:03.                      
N|               Close enough. Walt rises without waking his wife. He exits.          
O|
S|               INT. WHITE HOUSE - SPARE BEDROOM - NIGHT                             
O|
N,G|               We hear an o.s. SQUEAK-SQUEAK as we drift through this room.         
N,G|               We pass an empty crib, Pampers, a baby monitor still in its          
N|               box.  There’s going to be a new addition to the family.              
O|
N,G|               We come upon the source of the SQUEAKING. It’s Walt balanced         
N|               on a Lillian Vernon stair-stepper, just three easy payments          
N|               of $29.95. Walt plods up and down in the darkness like he’s          
N|               marching to Bataan.                                                  
O|
S|               INT. WHITE HOUSE - BATHROOM - NIGHT                                  
O|
N,G|               Walt sits down on the edge of the tub. We’re watching his            
N,G|               face in the bathroom mirror. He masturbates. Judging by his          
N|               expression, he might as well be waiting in line at the DMV.          
O|
N|               Walt double-takes, catching sight of himself. Distracted, he         
N|               examines the sallow bagginess under his eyes. He draws at            
N|               the loose skin under his chin.                                       
N|               Staring at himself long and hard, Walt loses his erection.           
N|               He gives up trying, pulls up his sweat pants.                        
O|
O|
O|                                                                      5.            
O|
